;;; A relation parameter its activator binds to `eq` heads a conclusion of one
;;; argument, and root stores `(eq a)` — M1f **W60**, register row
;;; `W60/eq-arity-conclusion` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), filed by S1f.5.9
;;; (T1f.5.9.1, 2026-11-09), `open` and owned by S1f.5.9.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule `r` whose parameter `?R` heads its conclusion, `(?R ?x)`, and its
;;; activator `(r eq)`: for every `p`, `r` concludes `(eq ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `eq` is the relation of equality, between exactly two values, whose facts
;;; are computed and never stored (M1f S1f.5.7, Q-M1e2.26, G-21), and the
;;; grammar refuses `(eq ?x)` as a conclusion. Written through the parameter
;;; and an activator the program states, it is refused at load, as the same
;;; parameter heading a premise is (`eq-arity-premise.ein`), and every
;;; command that loads the program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program loads and root stores `(eq a :rule r)`, a fact of `eq` at one
;;; argument (`ein saturate FILE --dump`), no conclusion checked; `eq` holds
;;; no candidate, so a state's `(model …)` does not name it, and R is two
;;; states, ∅ and `{(p z)}`, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE`
;;; at `ee3da657`, release and debug alike).

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a p")
(p a)
(hit z)
(rule r (?R)
  :match (p ?x)
  :assert (?R ?x)
  :why "{?x} is a p, so it stands in {?R}")
(r eq)
(query :goal (p ?x))
